``int()`` calls are no longer inferred as a constant the call would not return.
A second argument was dropped, so ``int('10', 2)`` was inferred as ``10`` instead
of ``2``, and any first argument that was not an ``int`` or a ``str`` fell through
to the value used for the no-argument form, so ``int(2.5)`` and ``int(b'12')``
were inferred as ``0``. Values that cannot be resolved, including the ones that
raise ``ValueError`` at runtime such as ``int('abc')``, are now left to the
regular call inference instead of being reported as ``0``.

Refs #3271
